op {
  graph_op_name: "EncodeJxl"
  in_arg {
    name: "image"
    description: <<END
Images to encode. Rank N >= 3 with shape `batch_dims + [height, width, channels]`.
END
  }
  out_arg {
    name: "contents"
    description: <<END
0-D or higher depending on input batch dimensions. JPEG XL-encoded image(s).
END
  }
  attr {
    name: "quality"
    description: <<END
JPEG-style quality factor in [0.0, 100.0]. Defaults to 95.0 to match
`encode_jpeg`. 100.0 encodes losslessly, 90.0 is visually lossless, and lower
values compress more.
END
  }
  attr {
    name: "effort"
    description: <<END
Effort level from 1 (fastest) to 9 (slowest/best compression).
END
  }
  summary: "JPEG XL-encode an image."
  description: <<END
`image` is a rank-N uint8, uint16, half, or float Tensor of shape `batch_dims + [height, width, channels]`
where `channels` is:

*   1: for grayscale.
*   3: for RGB.
*   4: for RGBA.

`quality` is mapped internally to a Butteraugli distance, using the same
mapping as `cjxl -q`: 100.0 maps to distance 0.0 (lossless modular encoding),
95.0 maps to distance 0.5 (default lossy VarDCT encoding), 90.0 maps to
distance 1.0, and 0.0 maps to distance 25.0. Note that quality factors are not
comparable across codecs; a given `quality` will not produce the same file size
or visual quality as the same value passed to `tf.io.encode_jpeg`.
END
}
